== Vision-Language Models for Dense Prediction and Segmentation

Large vision-language models have rapidly acquired the spatial reasoning capabilities needed for dense visual understanding. Liu et al. @liu2024llava introduced LLaVA, pairing a CLIP visual encoder with a large language model through a projection layer to establish visual instruction tuning as a scalable recipe for multimodal reasoning. You et al. @you2024ferret proposed Ferret, a hybrid region representation enabling referring and grounding at arbitrary granularity. Peng et al. @peng2024kosmos2 demonstrated with Kosmos-2 that spatial coordinates can be expressed as discrete location tokens within the language vocabulary, enabling grounded text generation without auxiliary detection heads. Chen et al. @chen2024spatialvlm endowed VLMs with quantitative spatial reasoning, training models to estimate metric distances and spatial relations from single images. These advances show that modern VLMs possess increasingly precise spatial understanding, a prerequisite for reasoning about where to place segmentation prompts.

A parallel line of work connects VLMs directly to pixel-level segmentation. Lai et al. @lai2024lisa introduced LISA, a reasoning segmentation framework where the language model emits a special token whose embedding is projected into SAM's prompt space, enabling end-to-end segmentation from complex queries. Yang et al. @yang2023setofmark offered a training-free alternative with Set-of-Mark prompting, overlaying numbered markers on SAM proposals so a frozen VLM can ground its answers by referring to specific regions. Ren et al. @ren2024pixellm proposed PixelLM for multi-target pixel reasoning, while Rasheed et al. @rasheed2024glamm presented GLaMM, a grounded conversational model coupling a multimodal LLM with a SAM-based pixel decoder trained on 810 million region annotations. Zhang et al. @zhang2024psalm unified referring, interactive, and semantic segmentation within a single language-model framework, and Zhang et al. @zhang2024llavagrounding connected LLaVA to a segmentation decoder for visually grounded dialogue.

Our V3 system departs from all of the above by deploying Qwen-VL @bai2025qwen25vl as an interactive segmentation policy that reasons about prompt placement across multiple turns. At each step the model observes the current image with overlaid prior prompts, performs chain-of-thought spatial reasoning, and emits the next click coordinate. This iterative, closed-loop formulation enables correction of earlier errors, a capability absent from single-pass architectures such as LISA and GLaMM, and achieves 0.853 Dice with an average of 1.4 clicks.
